The '''2026 UEFA Conference League final''' was the final match of the [[2025–26 UEFA Conference League]], the fifth season of Europe's tertiary club [[association football|football]] tournament organised by [[UEFA]], and the second season since it was renamed from the UEFA Europa Conference League to the [[UEFA Conference League]]. It was played at the [[Red Bull Arena (Leipzig)|Red Bull Arena]] in [[Leipzig]], Germany,<ref name="appointment"/> on 27 May 2026 between English club [[Crystal Palace F.C.|Crystal Palace]] and Spanish club [[Rayo Vallecano]].

Crystal Palace won the match 1–0 for their first UEFA Conference League title.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaconferenceleague/news/02a5-20ba50a7a602-983af582dc90-1000/ |title=Crystal Palace 1–0 Rayo Vallecano: Jean-Philippe Mateta wins Conference League for the Eagles |publisher=[[UEFA]] |date=27 May 2026 |access-date=27 May 2026}}</ref> As winners, they qualified for the league phase of the [[2026–27 UEFA Europa League]].<ref>{{cite web|url= https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/live/clypgp9wrp9t|title= Crystal Palace 1 Rayo Vallecano 0|date=27 May 2026|work=BBC Sport|accessdate=28 May 2026}}</ref>

==Background==
Crystal Palace, having [[Crystal Palace F.C. in European football|played in a major European competition]] for the first time, reached their first ever European final. They were aiming for their second major trophy, having won the [[FA Cup]] in [[2024–25 FA Cup|2025]], beating [[Manchester City F.C.|Manchester City]] in the final.<ref>{{cite news |date=17 May 2025 |title=Crystal Palace – 2024/25 FA Cup winners! |url=https://www.cpfc.co.uk/news/announcement/crystal-palace-2024-25-fa-cup-champions/ |access-date=27 May 2026 |publisher=Crystal Palace F.C.}}</ref> As winners of the FA Cup, they were initially given a [[2025–26 UEFA Europa League|Europa League]] league phase berth but due to [[Multi-club ownership (association football)|multi-club ownership]] restrictions regarding club stakeholder [[John Textor]] also having ownership of French team [[Olympique Lyonnais]] via [[Eagle Football Holdings]], Crystal Palace's European qualification berth was switched with [[Nottingham Forest F.C.|Nottingham Forest]]'s, who initially took England's Conference League berth, by UEFA to prevent clubs with the same owners competing in the same tournament, as Lyon finished in a higher [[2024–25 Ligue 1|league position in France]] than Palace [[2024–25 Premier League|did in England]].<ref>{{cite web |title=The CFCB First Chamber renders decision on Crystal Palace and Olympique Lyonnais |url=https://www.uefa.com/news-media/news/029b-1e361573fefc-94aa466245fc-1000--the-cfcb-first-chamber-renders-decision-on-crystal-palace-an/ |website=[[UEFA]] |access-date=11 July 2025}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2025-07-11 |title=Crystal Palace set to be replaced by Nottingham Forest in Europa League |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/articles/ckg5260zzp2o |access-date=2025-07-11 |website=BBC Sport |language=en-GB}}</ref>

Rayo Vallecano also reached their first ever European final, and were aiming for a first major title. Rayo Vallecano had previously qualified for the quarter-finals of the [[2000–01 UEFA Cup#Quarter-finals|2000–01 UEFA Cup]], losing 4–2 on aggregate to fellow Spanish side [[Deportivo Alavés|Alavés]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Alavés vs. Rayo Vallecano Match Report – Thursday March 8, 2001 (Leg 1) |url=https://fbref.com/en/matches/ff6ffb94/Alaves-Rayo-Vallecano-March-8-2001-UEFA-Cup |access-date=27 May 2026 |website=FBref}}</ref>

==Venue==
[[File:Leipzig stadium.jpg|thumb|[[Red Bull Arena (Leipzig)|Red Bull Arena]] in [[Leipzig]] hosted the final.]]

===Host selection===
On 17 May 2023, UEFA opened the bidding process for the final, which was held in parallel with that of the [[2027 UEFA Conference League final|2027 final]]. Interested bidders could bid for either one or both of the finals. Additionally, bidding associations could only be appointed one UEFA final in a given year. The proposed venues had to include natural grass and be ranked as a [[UEFA stadium categories|UEFA category four stadium]], with a gross capacity of 30,000 to 60,000 preferred. The bidding timeline was as follows:<ref name="invitation to bid">{{cite magazine |url=https://editorial.uefa.com/resources/0281-18125a4a50d7-237b59952ab8-1000/20230517_circular_2023_24_en.zip |title=Invitation to bid to host the UEFA Champions League Finals 2026 & 2027, UEFA Europa League Finals 2026 & 2027, UEFA Europa Conference League Finals 2026 & 2027 and UEFA Women's Champions League Finals 2026 & 2027 |magazine=UEFA Circular Letter |publisher=[[UEFA|Union of European Football Associations]] |number=24/2023 |date=17 May 2023 |access-date=22 May 2024}}</ref>

*17 May 2023: Applications formally invited
*17 July 2023: Closing date for registering intention to bid
*26 July 2023: Bid requirements made available to bidders
*15 November 2023: Submission of preliminary bid dossier
*21 February 2024: Submission of final bid dossier
*22 May 2024: Appointment of host

UEFA announced on 18 July 2023 that six associations had expressed interest in hosting the 2026 and 2027 finals during the first bidding process.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.uefa.com/news-media/news/0283-1885294a1bdd-b1bd50a82ae1-1000/ |title=Nine national associations interested in hosting UEFA club competition finals in 2026 and 2027 |publisher=[[UEFA]] |date=18 July 2023 |access-date=16 September 2025}}</ref>

